description:
Write the bookkeeping of a plugin host: which plugins run, in what order, what each one registered, and what happens when one is reloaded. Each plugin file is described by:

```cpp
struct PluginFile
{
    std::string name;                     // "fog"
    uint32_t apiVersion{ 1 };
    std::vector<std::string> requires_;   // names of plugins that must be running first
    std::vector<std::string> provides;    // ids it registers when it starts, e.g. "fog.ghost_stone"
};

class Host
{
public:
    explicit Host(uint32_t apiVersion);
    void addFile(const PluginFile& file);                  // a new file, or a new build replacing one with the same name
    std::vector<std::string> startAll();                   // start every plugin that is not running; returns log lines
    std::vector<std::string> reload(const std::string& name);
    std::vector<std::string> running() const;              // names, in the order they were started
    std::string providerOf(const std::string& id) const;   // "" if nobody provides it
};
```

**Starting.** `startAll` visits the plugins that are not running in **alphabetical** order and starts each one's requirements first (in the order listed, depth first). A plugin starts if every check passes, in this order:

1. its `apiVersion` equals the host's, else log `skip <name>: API version <v>, need <host>`;
2. every requirement exists and is running (or can be started now), else `skip <name>: needs <requirement>` for the first one that fails; reaching a plugin that is already being started in this chain is a cycle: `skip <name>: cycle`;
3. it registers its `provides` ids in order. Each must start with `<name>.` (else `skip <name>: <id> is not <name>.*`) and must not be registered already (else `skip <name>: <id> is taken`). If any registration fails, the ids it registered before the failure are removed again.

A plugin that starts logs `start <name>` and is added to the end of the running list. Each plugin is logged at most once per `startAll` (its first result).

**Reloading.** `reload(name)` stops the plugin and every running plugin that requires it, directly or indirectly, in **reverse start order**, logging `stop <x>` for each and removing everything each one registered. Then it calls `startAll` and appends its log. Reloading a plugin that is not running only runs `startAll`.

